\subsection{Cross-Seed Variance Decomposition}
\label{app:variance-decomposition}

To quantify whether performance variability is driven by random batch composition or seed-dependent model inference noise, we decompose total variance into intra-seed (batch) and inter-seed components across three seeds (42, 123, 456), each evaluated on 25 random batches per count level.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Count} & \textbf{Batch SD} & \textbf{Seed SD} & \textbf{Ratio} \\
\midrule
4 & 0.0889 & 0.0122 & 53.5 \\
8 & 0.0820 & 0.0098 & 70.0 \\
16 & 0.0626 & 0.0270 & 5.4 \\
32 & 0.0462 & 0.0091 & 26.0 \\
40 & 0.0421 & 0.0019 & 474.4 \\
64 & 0.0245 & 0.0022 & 119.8 \\
80 & 0.0001 & 0.0088 & $<$0.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Variance decomposition across count levels on FewRel. Batch SD is the root-mean intra-seed variance across 25 random type batches (averaged over 3 seeds). Seed SD is the standard deviation of the 3 per-seed means. Ratio = mean batch variance / seed variance. At count 80, only one batch composition exists (the full schema), so batch variance vanishes by construction.}
\label{tab:variance_decomposition}
\end{table}

Intra-batch variance dominates inter-seed variance by 5--474$\times$ across count levels 4--64 (mean 125$\times$), confirming that random batch composition is the primary source of performance variability, not seed-dependent randomness in model inference. The wide ratio range reflects variation in how tightly seed means cluster at each count level; even the minimum ratio (5.4$\times$ at count 16) indicates batch composition contributes the majority of observed variance. At count 80, the ratio inverts because the full schema admits only one batch composition, leaving model inference noise as the sole variance source (seed SD = 0.88\,pp).

